# ComposePlatform Corpus Conventions [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

> The readable narration of how the ComposePlatform LTS corpus is organized. The machine-readable
> source of truth is `profile/composeplatform-profile.yaml` (the taxonomy + partition registry) and,
> for the base grammar, the FactLattice domain profile `platform-composeio.lts`. This document exists
> so a human (David) can read the conventions without parsing YAML, and so a successor Architect can
> orient before authoring — but it DEFERS to the profile yaml for every mechanical value (Haven D8:
> conventions as a normal readable spec, the registry as the queryable artifact). Converted from the
> legacy SMD `99-composeio-conventions.smd`; the SMD-era prefix table is superseded by the profile.

---

## 1. The Registry Lives in the Profile, Not Here

### [PLT-CONV-REGISTRY] The Machine-Readable Registry Is profile/composeplatform-profile.yaml

- definition: The authoritative taxonomy — the domains (`plt`, `io`, `ux`, `app.console`, `app.composer`, `app.monitor`, `svc`, `wid`), their corpus roots, the layer/family axes, and the per-corpus sub-directory layout (`specs`/`decisions`/`artifacts`) — is registered ONCE in `profile/composeplatform-profile.yaml`. This document narrates it; it never re-declares a value.
- rule: when a value here and in the yaml disagree, the YAML wins. Add new domains/prefixes/verbs to the yaml; update this prose to match.
- rationale: structure survives the boundary; prose leaks. A queryable registry is the carrier a summarizer cannot flatten ([DX-STRUCTURE-SURVIVES]). The readable spec is a courtesy for the human reviewer, not a parser input.
- points-at: `profile/composeplatform-profile.yaml`
- source-of-truth: [DEC-COMPOSEPLATFORM-REFRAME]
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

---

## 2. Multi-Corpus Partition — IDs Are Unique WITHIN a Corpus

### [PLT-CONV-PARTITION] Each Domain Is Its Own Corpus; IDs Do Not Globally Collide

- definition: ComposePlatform is a MULTI-corpus lattice, not one flat pool. The domain suffix in the profile (`io`, `ux`, `app.console`, ...) selects a corpus root; each corpus has independent prefix registries. A node ID is unique WITHIN its corpus, never globally — a merged single graph would clobber.
- rule: intra-corpus references MUST resolve (a forward dangle within a corpus is a defect). Cross-corpus references are EXPECTED and become the thin routing layer — they are not dangles to fix, they are the edges between corpora (MAGMA multi-corpus partition model, Haven D5).
- linter-guidance: run the linter per-corpus (a single bare directory is linted recursively as ONE corpus). Cross-corpus forward refs are warnings/expected, not errors.
- see-also: [PLT-CONV-XREF]
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

### [PLT-CONV-NUMBERING] The Numbered-Layering Convention Within a Corpus

- definition: within a corpus, `00` = foundation/overview, `0x` = architecture/substrate, ascending by dependency depth, `99` = conventions, plus a glossary node (`10-glossary` at the platform root). A number collision at copy-in is resolved Haven-D6-style (e.g. the two `00-composer-*` specs → `00-composer-overview` keeps `00`, `00-composer-design-principles` becomes `00a-`).
- rule: renumber the FILENAME only; node IDs and content stay byte-stable across a copy/renumber.
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

---

## 3. Document-Type Tags & Node Prefixes

### [PLT-CONV-DOCTYPE] Valid Document-Type Tags

- definition: a document header carries a bracketed `[DOCTYPE:ID]` tag. Valid doctypes = FactLattice core plus the corpus set: `SPEC` (technical specification), `AMEND` (amendment to an existing spec, uses the `amends:` frontmatter key), `SYSTEM` (system architecture or conventions — this file, foundation, glossary).
- linter-guidance: registered in the profile `document_tags`. A tag outside the set is an S001 error.
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

### [PLT-CONV-PREFIX] Node-ID Prefixes Are Corpus-Scoped

- definition: a node ID's prefix identifies its document-of-origin and semantic family (`CXC-` = ComposeUX Composer, `DASH-` = runtime dashboard, `REN-` = widget renderers, `PLT-` = platform-wide, `DX-` = design-excellence edges, `INSIGHT-` = the insights ledger, `DEC-` = a decision node). The full prefix registry is in the profile yaml; the SMD-era table in the legacy conventions is superseded by it.
- linter-guidance: an unregistered prefix is S007/S008 — a WARNING, not an error. New corpora add their prefix(es) to the profile yaml.
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

---

## 4. Cross-Reference & Relationship Syntax

### [PLT-CONV-XREF] Bracketed Document & Node References

- definition: qualified `[DOCTYPE:ID]` references name a document (`[SPEC:CXC-ARCH]`, `[SYSTEM:PLT-FOUNDATION]`); bare `[NODE-ID]` references name a node in this or another corpus (`[DX-NARROW-WAIST]`, `[DEC-COMPOSEPLATFORM-REFRAME]`).
- linter-guidance: a qualified reference to a registered doctype is valid even when unresolvable in-corpus (it is a cross-corpus routing edge). A bare cross-corpus node ref with a registered prefix is a WARNING if unresolvable in-corpus, never an error.
- convention: references in prose and edges MAY carry a parenthetical context: `- depends-on: [SPEC:CIO-APP] (transport interface)`.
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

### [PLT-CONV-RELVERB] Relationship-Verb Vocabulary

- definition: an optional `## Relationships` block carries inline `[A] VERB [B]` triples. Core verbs: `DEPENDS_ON` (A mechanically requires B), `RELATED_TO` (generic association). Domain verbs: `USES`, `EXEMPLIFIES`, `REALIZES`, `UPHOLDS`, `DOCUMENTS`. Typed EDGE properties on a node (`see-also:`, `part-of:`, `relates-to:`, `depends-on:`, `re-derive-via:`, `spawns:`, `governs:`, `points-at:`) are the more common carrier.
- linter-guidance: the verb set is registered in the profile `relationship_verbs`; an unregistered verb is a WARNING (RV01).
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

---

## 5. Frontmatter & Footer

### [PLT-CONV-FRONTMATTER] Frontmatter Keys

- definition: every spec opens with a `---` frontmatter block. Common keys: `scope`, `audience`, `boundary`, `not-this`, `domain` (a `PLT.<SUB>` / `IO.<SUB>` / `UX.<SUB>` etc. suffix that MUST match the corpus), `confidentiality` (`public` | `nda-shareable` | `internal-only`), `artifact-readiness` (`educational` | `system-design` | `code-generation` | `design-review` | `concept-capture`), `depends-on`, `created`, and (amendments only) `amends`.
- linter-guidance: the readiness/confidentiality enums are registered in the profile; both file-path and bracketed-ID `depends-on:` formats are accepted.
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

### [PLT-CONV-FOOTER] Document Footer

- definition: specs MAY close with `**Version**` / `**Created**` / `**Status**` and optional `**Intelligence**` / `**Deployment Target**` fields.
- linter-guidance: missing `**Version**`/`**Status**` are WARNINGS, not errors (profile `linter_guidance.footer`).
- status: operational
- part-of: [SYSTEM:PLT-CONVENTIONS]

